# Overview
#
# Reports `not only` and `not just`, mostly in the paired form `not only
# X but Y` (`the cache holds not only rules but results`). The rule
# reports the contracted spellings too (`isn't just`, `doesn't only`).
# The paired form presents two facts as a contrast, so the reader must
# work out that both hold. The fix makes the direct claim (`the cache
# holds rules and results`) and gives a needed contrast its own
# sentence. The prose skill bans the paired form. The rule sits at
# Recommended, because an unpaired `not just` also states a correction
# that the reader needs. The rule passes that form only after a comma or
# an open paren.
#
# Expression
#
# `which` holds `only` and `just`. The first branch reports `not only`
# or `not just` unless a comma or an open paren comes before it. Weir
# has no lookbehind, so the branch consumes the token before the space
# ahead of `not`, and the filter narrows the finding back to the phrase.
# A comma fails that token, and an open paren leaves no space before
# `not`.
#
# The `just` in `which` carries a guard that skips `any`. A negated word
# after a space needs a word to consume, so `just` right before a comma
# or a period passes (`the hook is not just, as some say, a linter`).
#
# The first branch needs a token before `not`, so a sentence that opens
# with `Not only` counts through the second branch or the third. The
# second branch needs the helping verb that this form moves ahead of the
# subject (`Not only does the cache hold rules`).
#
# The third branch finds the paired form anywhere, after a comma too, by
# matching `but` up to eight words after `only` or `just`. A list picks
# its longest match and never backtracks, so a gap list followed by
# `but` swallows the `but`. Each gap length is therefore its own
# alternative that ends in `but`. The last word before `but` can carry a
# comma (`Not only the cache, but the log grows`).
#
# `short` holds the contractions that ProseNegativeContraction reports
# other than `can't`, such as `isn't` and `don't`. A contraction before
# `only` or `just` makes the same move as its full form (`it isn't just
# a cache`, `don't just run make`), so the fourth branch reports it. The
# branch has no guard for a comma, because the verb of a contraction
# sits between a comma and the negation (`Read the docs, don't just run
# make`). The first branch reports the full form of that sentence for
# the same reason.
#
# `can't` stays out of `short`, because its full form `cannot` holds no
# separate `not`, and the rule passes `cannot just`. Rarer contractions
# such as `mightn't` and `shan't` stay out too, so `mightn't just`
# passes but `might not just` counts. The fourth branch needs `only` or
# `just` right after the contraction, so the question `isn't it just a
# cache` passes but `is it not just a cache` counts.
#
# The third branch takes a contraction from `short` in place of `not`
# too, so the paired form draws the same finding in both spellings. Only
# the first and fourth branches read `which`, so the paired form counts
# even with `any` (`this isn't just any file but the root config`).
#
# `more than just` makes the same move too (`it is more than just a
# cache`), so the last branch reports it.
#
# False Positives
#
# A `not just` or a `not only` after a comma or an open paren passes.
# That form is a trailing correction (`Name the log events, not just the
# sentinel`, `(not just the prune)`), and it names a contrast the reader
# needs.
#
# Without the comma or the paren, the same correction still matches
# (`Name the log events and not just the sentinel`). The rule accepts
# that misfire.
#
# `just !any` skips the idiom `not just any file`, and it skips the same
# idiom after a contraction (`this isn't just any file`). The glued
# hyphen in `not just-in-time` already fails the whitespace match.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseJust and ProseMinimizer skip `just` after a negation and after
# `than`, so neither reports a `just` that this rule reports (`do not
# just run make`, `don't just run make`, `does more than just run the
# tests`).
#
# ProseNegativeContraction reports each contraction in `short`, and its
# fix writes the full form. When `only` or `just` follows the
# contraction, this rule reports both spellings, so `don't just run
# make` shows its finding beside the contraction finding on the first
# pass.
#
# ProseDoubleNegative skips `not` and each contraction in `short` before
# `only` or `just`, so it passes `don't just run make without reading
# the docs`, which this rule reports. Its `short` pattern copies the
# `short` list here, so change the two together.
#
# ProseContrastiveNegation reports a clause that denies one framing
# before it asserts another (`the hook isn't just a linter, it's a
# gate`). When the denial holds `just` or `only`, the sentence draws a
# finding from both rules, and no guard separates the two.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 33 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 18 of 20
# sampled hits the direct claim said the same. Of the 20, 15 were `not
# only` or `not just`, 3 were `more than just`, and 2 were a contraction
# before `just`. In both misfires, `just` meant `simply` before a verb
# (`why not just define the trait`, `you won't just know what to bring
# into scope`).
#
# On 5 of the lines that the rule hits in the measure.py corpus, `just`
# sits between `not` or a contraction and a verb. Of those 5, 3 are the
# paired form (`doesn't just cut the load on the scheduler, it can also
# save money`), and the other 2 are the misfires of the sample. A guard
# for a verb after `just` drops those 2 lines and 2 of the 3 paired
# lines. The rule therefore has no such guard, and it reports both
# spellings before a verb.
expr aux [does, do, did, is, are, was, were, can, could, will, would, has, have, should, must]
expr which [only, (just !any)]
expr short [don't, doesn't, didn't, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, couldn't, won't, wouldn't, shouldn't, mustn't, hasn't, haven't, hadn't, needn't]
expr last [*, (*,)]
expr tail [(@last but), (* @last but), (* * @last but), (* * * @last but), (* * * * @last but), (* * * * * @last but), (* * * * * * @last but), (* * * * * * * @last but)]
expr main [<(!(,)( )not @which), (not @which)>, (not only @aux), ([not, @short] [only, just] @tail), (@short @which), (more than just)]
let message "`not only` or `not just`. Make the direct claim, and give a needed contrast its own sentence."
let description "Flags `not only`, `not just`, and their contracted forms such as `isn't just`, except an unpaired correction after a comma or an open paren."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The cache holds rules and results."
allows "Not just any file can be opened this way."
allows "The runtime uses ahead-of-time compilation, not just-in-time."
allows "Name the log events, not just the sentinel."
allows "Refusing the run (not just the prune) also prevents the race."
allows "This isn't just any file."
allows "You can't just use a container for this."
